{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.Resurgence.StokesPhaseSignChirality","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.Resurgence.StokesPhaseSignChirality","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Analysis.SpecialFunctions.Trigonometric.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.Data.Complex.Basic","lean:module:QOU.BraidsAndKnots.S5SO3FiberAction"],"declarations":9,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Resurgence.chirality_sign_is_real_pi_phase","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Resurgence.chirality_sign_is_real_pi_phase","kind":"theorem","line":148,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Resurgence/StokesPhaseSignChirality.lean#L148-L155","doc":"**The chirality sign is the real π-phase `(-1)^{n(λ)}` with square `+1`** —\ncomposing the two corpus facts `deltaSign_eq_nStat_parity` (the a-function-parity\nidentity) and `deltaSign_sq_one`.  A π-monodromy real ±1, categorically distinct\nfrom the π/2 imaginary Stokes discontinuity of Part 1. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Resurgence.no_imaginary_phase_sources_deltaSign","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Resurgence.no_imaginary_phase_sources_deltaSign","kind":"theorem","line":129,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Resurgence/StokesPhaseSignChirality.lean#L129-L137","doc":"**No bar-covariant imaginary Stokes phase supplies the Δ_λ chirality sign.**\nAny sign imprinted by such a phase is bar-odd (Part 2 hypothesis); the physical\nΔ_λ sign is not; therefore the ±i phase does not source it.  This is the honest\nnegative resolving the `StokesPhaseSign` carrier: the sign horn is NOT closed by\nthe Stokes phase. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Resurgence.deltaSign_not_barOdd","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Resurgence.deltaSign_not_barOdd","kind":"theorem","line":117,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Resurgence/StokesPhaseSignChirality.lean#L117-L127","doc":"**The physical Δ_λ chirality sign is NOT bar-odd** (proved by explicit\ncounterexample).  The chiral pair `(5) ↔ (1⁵)` is a genuine conjugate pair\n(`(5).conjugate = (1⁵) ≠ (5)`) whose signs are EQUAL — `deltaSign (1⁵) = +1 =\ndeltaSign (5)` — not opposite.  So the bar-involution does not flip it, and the\n`bar_flip` requirement of a ±i Stokes phase fails on the real target. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Resurgence.BarOdd","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Resurgence.BarOdd","kind":"def","line":110,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Resurgence/StokesPhaseSignChirality.lean#L110-L115","doc":"A sign assignment is **bar-odd** when it flips under the bar-involution\n(partition conjugation `λ ↦ λ'`) on every chiral (non-self-conjugate) pair.\nThis is exactly `StokesPhaseSign.bar_flip`: a bar-covariant ±i Stokes phase must\nimprint a bar-odd sign. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Resurgence.stokesDiscontinuity_sq","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Resurgence.stokesDiscontinuity_sq","kind":"theorem","line":92,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Resurgence/StokesPhaseSignChirality.lean#L92-L99","doc":"**The ±i discontinuity is a π/2 (quarter-turn) monodromy: its square is a\nNEGATIVE real.**  `(2πi·ρ)² = −(2πρ)²`.  Contrast Part 3: the chirality sign\nsquares to `+1` (a π monodromy).  The two monodromy orders differ. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Resurgence.stokesDiscontinuity_ne_zero","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Resurgence.stokesDiscontinuity_ne_zero","kind":"theorem","line":80,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Resurgence/StokesPhaseSignChirality.lean#L80-L90","doc":"**A nonzero residue gives a nonzero (purely imaginary) discontinuity.**  So\nthe Stokes jump genuinely exists — it is not a removable artifact. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Resurgence.stokesDiscontinuity_im","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Resurgence.stokesDiscontinuity_im","kind":"theorem","line":75,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Resurgence/StokesPhaseSignChirality.lean#L75-L78","doc":"The imaginary part is the full magnitude `2πρ`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Resurgence.stokesDiscontinuity_re_zero","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Resurgence.stokesDiscontinuity_re_zero","kind":"theorem","line":69,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Resurgence/StokesPhaseSignChirality.lean#L69-L73","doc":"**The Stokes discontinuity is purely imaginary** — zero real part.  This is\nthe `±i` signature of the alien derivative: the `2πi` Laplace-contour residue\nhas no real component. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Resurgence.stokesDiscontinuity","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Resurgence.stokesDiscontinuity","kind":"def","line":64,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Resurgence/StokesPhaseSignChirality.lean#L64-L67","doc":"The Stokes / alien discontinuity value across a simple Borel singularity with\nreal residue `ρ`: the Laplace-contour monodromy `2πi·ρ`. ","signatureChunk":0}]}